+        "mini-ball position updates scaled by dt60",
+        "updateParticles(): p.vy gravity and p.life decay scaled by dt60",
+        "simulatePreviewArc(): time-based simulation replaces fixed-frame loop"
+      ]
+    },
+    {
+      "file": "tests/tests/b01_ball_physics.test.js",
+      "finding": "22 call sites of b.update() with no arguments — dt60 is undefined in the test environment. Tests call b.update() directly without any dt60 argument. At 60Hz in real game, dt60=1.0 on most frames so NaN doesn't manifest widely; at the exact first frame dt60=0 gives 0 gravitational increment which is acceptable. But individual tests like 'Ball.update — teleportCooldown decrements' expect teleportCooldown=9 after one update (was -=1, now -=undefined → NaN). On 60Hz hardware these NaN values propagate and accumulate — the test suite would fail on real hardware or if the tests ever ran multiple updates without the undefined-correcting behavior of JS. The tests for gravity expect vy=0.18 after one update, but with dt60=1.0 and GRAVITY=0.18 this would pass at 60Hz. However dt60=0 on first rAF frame causes 0 gravitational increment on the very first update call — some tests may pass only because the undefined dt60 silently coerces to NaN in ways the test assertions don't fully exercise.",
