+      "file": "SPEC.md physics constants",
+      "finding": "SPEC.md specifies GRAVITY=0.15 and BOUNCE_DAMPING=0.75 but code has GRAVITY=0.18, FRICTION=0.995, BOUNCE=0.65. The test file b01_ball_physics.test.js already diverges from both spec and code (GRAVITY=0.18 in tests, not 0.15). The delta-time fix uses GRAVITY=0.18*60=10.8 px/sec² as stated in the index.html comment; this was not derivable from the spec which says 0.15. After the fix, the effective GRAVITY_PER_SEC is consistent regardless of display Hz, but the tests use 0.18 as a per-frame constant without delta-time adjustment."
+    },
+    {
+      "file": "Overall assessment",
+      "finding": "The delta-time fix is correct physics engineering. Tests are the coverage gap: they were written assuming frame-rate-absolute physics and need migrating to pass explicit dt60 values or accept normalized time-based assertions. Real-world behavior at 60Hz is preserved because dt60=1.0 on most frames and the first-frame dt=0 case is handled. The tests would mostly pass at 60Hz but fail at any other refresh rate. The principal risks are: (1) tests failing on actual hardware with variable refresh, (2) no test covers high-Hz behavior, (3) no test covers the dt=0 first-frame edge case, (4) no test covers dt>1.0 (120Hz+/144Hz+)."
+    }
